WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.610
Hello, everybody.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.910
My name is Ryan Johnson and I'm
a data science advisor here,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.110
At Posit.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.290
And welcome to this month's
Enterprise Community meetup,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.720
where we'll discuss
an end to end data

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.960
science workflow
using Posit team.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.180
As a reminder, this will
be a recurring event

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.550
on the last Wednesday
of every month,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.770
and we hope it'll
serve as a good primer

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.890
for those new to Posit
team, but also helps

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.340
current Posit team users become
more familiar with our tools.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.800
Now, every month we will
cover a different topic

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.760
and highlight all three
tools within Posit Team.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.750
We have Posit Workbench,
Posit Connect and Posit Package

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.700
Manager.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.770
So during and after
each presentation

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.020
there will be a few members
of the Posit team in the chat,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.710
to address any questions
you have about our tools,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.020
workflows, licensing
and anything else

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.060
you may be curious about.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.450
So this month we have a pretty
exciting topic for everyone,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.540
especially those within the
life sciences, healthcare

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.370
and pharmaceutical space.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.620
So we are going
to use Posit Team

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.250
to create a Shiny application
of clinical trial data

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:12.390
in our all while discussing
best practices for working

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.847
within what's known as
a validated environment.

00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:17.430
Now, as we go through
today's session,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.510
I'll be sure to stop and note
various ways Posit team can

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.820
help support a validated
compute environment

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.030
and encourage good
coding practices.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.830
So let's get started.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.930
Now I first want to make
sure everyone on the call

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.890
has a solid, high level
understanding of Posit Team.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.990
So starting off here
at the very top,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.960
we have our data
scientist and analyst.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.410
These are the folks writing
code and creating insights

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:48.180
using Posit Workbench, and they
can use whatever language they

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.570
want, including R or python,
as well as any development

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.050
environment they choose,
including the RStudio IDEl

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.340
Jupyter lab, Jupyter
Notebooks and VS Code.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.000
Now for the R developers they
may be creating insights

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.990
like Shiny applications.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:11.310
Pins RMarkdown documents or
APIs using the Plumber package.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.290
And for Python developers,
they have a home

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.410
to create interactive
applications using things

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:21.240
like streamlit, dash, bokeh, Shiny.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.330
They can also create documents
using Jupyter Notebooks or even

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:30.450
Quarto and APIs using
Flask and Fast API.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.060
Now, once the developers
create content,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.290
they need a way to share it with
the people that need to see,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.220
including decision makers,
coworkers, clients, or maybe

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.810
even just friends and family.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.090
And so that's the
role of Posit Connect,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.420
which is our professional
publishing platform.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.210
And finally, we have
Posit Package Manager,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.540
which helps organize
and centralize

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.440
the great open source R and
Python packages your team uses

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.602
as well as host and distribute
any internally developed R

00:03:01.602 --> 00:03:03.060
and Python packages
you've created.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.800
So for today's
session, we are going

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.800
to start within
Posit Workbench

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.680
and we will leverage
the RStudio IDE

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.410
to create a Shiny application
in R using clinical trial data.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.800
And then we'll publish this
application to Posit Connect

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.160
all within the context of
a validated environment.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.550
Now just for awareness,
the clinical trial data

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.990
that we'll use today
is publicly available.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.490
It's been properly
de-identified and cleansed

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.950
for demonstration purposes.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.740
So before we actually
dive into this workflow,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.840
let's take a few moments
just to talk more

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.600
about this clinical
trial data set.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.450
Now, what is it?

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.570
Where does it come from.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:53.220
Now as a high level
overview, clinical trials,

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.550
they are conducted usually
to assess the efficacy

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.070
of some disease intervention.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:04.080
So during the trial, data
will be collected, organized

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.870
and eventually that data
will find itself inside

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:12.480
of an analysis ready data set
called an ADaM or analysis data

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.730
model data set.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.620
So these atom data
sets are usually

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.860
what's submitted to various
regulatory agencies,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.420
such as the Food and Drug
Administration, or FDA.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.120
So here's actually
a quick glimpse

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.500
into what this example
ADaM data set looks like.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.450
Now, we are only going
to focus on the columns

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.550
that you see here,
which include things

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:40.800
like subject, ID number, the
treatment group, age, BMI,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.490
height, weight and
years of education.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.910
So these subject level
variables are usually

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.700
found within a specific
type of ADaM data,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:56.280
so-called a subject
level or ADSL data set.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.380
Now while this data is not
particularly large, it does.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.190
Contain numerous
descriptive columns.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:03.010
And it can be a
challenge to create

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.630
static insights such as plots
and tables to explore them all.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:11.020
So for this reason, we will
create an interactive web

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.930
application using the
Shiny R package, which

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:15.700
I'm showing here.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.740
So this application
will allow viewers

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.260
to explore the
data for themselves

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.510
and compare various
subject level

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.880
variables between
the three treatments

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.650
groups, which we'll talk about
a little bit more in a bit.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:33.280
So to start, let's
open in RStudio session

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.300
within Posit Workbench.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.020
So here we have in
Posit Workbench.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.190
On the right hand side.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.700
You can see some of the
projects I've been working on.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:46.120
But we'll go ahead
and click New session.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.280
And you can see
right off the bat

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.310
that I have a choice between
Jupyter Notebooks, Jupyter

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.300
Lab, RStudio Pro and VS Code.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.430
So for today, we're
going to select RStudio.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.210
Now, before I
start this session,

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.510
I want to briefly describe our
demo computing environment here

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.980
at Posit.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:10.180
So we host Posit Workbench
within a clustered environment

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.910
which is managed by a
tool called Kubernetes,

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.710
which you can see here.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.530
So for validated
compute environments,

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:21.760
it's often necessary that
certain aspects of this RStudio

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.570
session are controlled for
including what R version is

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:31.330
being used, which R or system
packages come preinstalled.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.810
And so that's where tools
like Docker come into play.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.060
And here at the
very bottom, you can

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.570
see that I have a choice
for various Docker images

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:43.960
I can deploy within, and
this can be customized

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.670
to your team's needs.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:48.370
So for this demo, I'm just going
to stick with the default image

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:51.470
right here and we'll go ahead
and click Start session.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:59.780
So while this is booting up,
before we do any analysis,

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.620
it's really important that
we establish a new RStudio

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.120
project for this
clinical trial analysis.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.790
So this will provide us
with a working directory

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:14.030
for this project and
only this project.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.270
So this helps ensure that
the analysis we do here

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.790
that they won't impact
anything else on our system

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:21.500
or another project.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.990
And this is generally
considered good practice

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.480
for not just clinical
trial data, but really

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.090
all data science projects.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.730
So to create a project, we
have a few different options.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.150
We can go to File at
the top of my screen

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:37.010
and select New Project.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.770
There's also a button right
underneath the Edit button

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.030
down here.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.250
Or if you go to the right
hand side of your screen,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:45.218
you can see project none.

00:07:45.218 --> 00:07:47.510
And this is actually a good
way of knowing that I'm not

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.365
currently within a project.

00:07:49.365 --> 00:07:50.740
So let's go ahead
and create one.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.545
I'm going to click on this
button and select New Project.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.820
So this is going to be
a brand new project.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.220
So we're going to go ahead
and create a new directory.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:07.150
But I do have a choice to create
a project within an existing

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.420
directory or pull on a
project from version control.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.200
But we'll stick with new
directory, select New project,

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:17.850
the first option and
the directory name.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:21.520
I'll just say test_ADSL_analysis.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.100
I'll place

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.240
This directory as a project within
under my Posit project's

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.500
directory.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.960
And then you'll see two
boxes right here, create,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:35.500
Git repository and use
renv with this project.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.299
So I'm going to
check those boxes

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:41.320
and I'm going to
hit Create project.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.620
So while this is booting up, a
quick note on those two boxes

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:45.820
that we just checked.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.660
So to ensure reproducibility
for your project,

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:52.960
it's always a good idea
to use version control

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.570
and to initialize the
project with something

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.310
known as renv.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:59.650
Now, once we finish
our workflow today,

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.470
we'll actually go back
and discuss renv

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.640
in more detail and how it can
be used to record our project's

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.740
package environment.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:10.960
All right, so here we are
now within our new project,

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:14.410
and you can see the name of
it in the top right corner.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:17.140
So first step in our
workflow is to upload

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.870
that ADaM ADSL
dataset, which

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:23.800
can be found right here on the
see this website or the GitHub

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:24.550
page.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:30.220
So this is going to be the data
set right here at adsl.xpt.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.280
So I've downloaded this
file to my local machine

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.430
and to upload it
to Posit Workbench.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:41.920
All we have to do is select
this upload button, Choose File

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:47.890
and I'll select adsl.xpt,
hit Open and OK.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:49.840
And now we can see
the file right here,

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.170
the bottom in our
files directory.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.840
So now we have the data
in our environment.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.560
We need to install some
packages, some R packages that

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:03.430
will be used for the creation
of our Shiny application.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:06.670
Now, in a validated
environment, developers

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.970
are often limited to a
select number of packages

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:15.280
that their team has approved
for clinical data analysis.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.140
So the question
really becomes, how

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:22.090
do I make sure I use packages
from a validated repository?

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:25.720
Well, enter Posit
Package Manager.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:30.730
So here in our demo environment
of Posit Package mMnager,

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:34.540
you can see I have a few
customized repositories

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:37.090
over here on the left
hand side of your screen,

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:41.470
including right down here a
validated repository which

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.900
includes packages that
we have classified

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.300
as validated for clinical work.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.520
Now this is just an
example repository

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:52.450
to demonstrate that it's
possible to create customized

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:56.610
repositories depending
on your team's needs.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:57.110
All right.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:00.370
So we can start exploring
these various packages,

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:02.500
but how do we
actually install them

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.650
from this repository
into RStudio

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.020
session and Posit Workbench?

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.390
So to do this, we're
going to select the Setup

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:13.030
tab at the top of
your screen, and I'm

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.490
going to scroll down to
the bottom right here.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.560
And we see using packages
inside the RStudio IDE

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:21.560
and you have the choice.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:26.050
You can change the repository
URL using the global options

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.570
or you can set it
more programmatically

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:32.620
by running the code
right here at the very bottom.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.080
So I'm going to copy
this code and I'm

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.780
going to come back
into Posit Workbench

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.260
and I'm going to paste it
into my R profile script

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.560
that you see right here.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:45.160
So when I click
on this and we're

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.990
going to add a few
new lines here,

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:50.680
I wrote a comment to myself
saying validated repo

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:53.620
and I'm going to paste that
URL that I just copied,

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:57.100
which you can see here is
that validated repository

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.740
on Posit Package Manager.

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.930
So the .R profile
script will always

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:05.200
be run as soon as you
start a new R session.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.660
So I'm going to go
ahead and save this file

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:11.450
and I'm going to restart R
by clicking session restart.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:16.840
R. so if I close
at this .R profile

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.140
and coming to my console, I
can check my active repository

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:24.640
by running options
repos and hit Enter.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.670
And you can see that I have
my validated rePository

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:28.800
ready to be used.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.290
And to install packages from.

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:40.030
All right, so once we have
all that we can, well for our

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.740
Shiny application,
we actually need

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.850
to install some of
the packages first.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:46.660
So before I actually bring
in the Shiny application,

