within the RStudio IDE

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:23.090
running on Posit Workbench.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:27.980
So select Run app, and I'll open
up my viewer pane down here.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.330
And so this is the
Simple application

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:38.950
that explores the subject
level variables in this study,

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:42.100
and it compares them between
the various treatment arms.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:45.735
So here we have those
that were given a placebo.

00:14:45.735 --> 00:14:47.110
In the middle, we
have those that

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:51.280
were given a low dose of a
drug known as Xanomeline,

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:53.290
and then another treatment
group that was given

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.900
a high dose of Xanomeline.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:01.210
And on the y axis, we had the
various subject level variables

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:05.320
that we can explore,
such as age, baseline

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.990
BMI, baseline height,
baseline weight.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:15.150
And we have years of education.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.430
So now that we've built
the Shiny application

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:20.040
within our validated
environment,

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.830
it's now time to publish
it, to Posit Connect,

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:25.620
so that we can easily share
it with the people that

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:27.480
need to see it.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:31.560
So for instructions on how to
publish a Shiny application

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.333
from within the
RStudio IDE, I'm

00:15:34.333 --> 00:15:36.000
actually going to
enlist the help of one

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:40.350
of our jumpstart examples on
the Posit Connect home page.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:45.050
So let me switch over to Posit
Connect in our home page.

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:49.350
Here I can select, publish and
I can go to jump start examples.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.920
And you can see right here I
have a portfolio dashboard,

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.650
Shiny application.

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:59.070
So we're not actually going
to use this specific app

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.400
within this example, but we
will use the instructions

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.920
for how to publish to
Posit Connect, which

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.750
starts on step five.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:10.260
So we'll start to get started
using portfolio dashboard

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:16.080
and then I'll skip forward to
step five, which is publish.

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:18.480
So it tells us that
we need to push

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.300
the publish button,
this little blue button

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.030
from within the RStudio IDE.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:26.250
And if publishing for
the very first time,

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.500
you'll actually
need to grab the URL

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:30.190
for your Posit
Connect server.

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:33.230
So I'll just copy that to
have it on my clipboard.

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:33.730
All right.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:34.980
So let's go ahead and do this.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.440
So we'll come back to Posit
Workbench, where I have

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:38.980
my Shiny application running.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:41.430
Go ahead and stop it.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:43.210
And here's our Shiny application.

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:44.470
This app.R script.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:48.150
And right at the top, we see
that blue publishing button.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.820
So I'll go ahead
and click on this.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:53.580
And you can see I'm publishing
this for the very first time,

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.320
so I'll select Next.

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:58.613
Now I'm going to publish,
To Posit Connect,

00:16:58.613 --> 00:17:00.030
and here's where
you want to paste

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.310
in the address for your
connect server, which

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.980
you can see for us it's
actually auto populated.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:07.619
So I'll select Next.

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:09.990
And it's going to get this
little pop up menu right here

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:10.713
to connect.

00:17:10.713 --> 00:17:11.880
And I see what I want to do.

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:15.480
So select Connect
successfully activated token

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.660
so I can close out of this
and connect my account.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.810
And now you can see the Posit
connect instance over here

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.540
in the top right quadrant.

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:28.190
We can give our application, our
Shiny application a title.

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.170
I'll just leave it as
a default right here.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:31.670
And this is where
you want to choose

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:34.290
what's going to go into
the deployment bundle.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:38.130
And so really we only need
this ADSL data set as well as

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:39.930
the code for the
Shiny application,

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:45.270
which is showing over here
and then we'll hit Publish.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.060
And so once we do this,
we'll get this deploy tab

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:52.140
that opens up and RStudio pretty
much takes care of this for us.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.730
So what it's doing
right now, it's

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.740
actually capturing
my environment.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.650
So it's looking to see what
R version am I using,

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:02.760
what packages am I using, what
versions of those packages

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.490
and some other information
about my environment and then

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:08.970
replicates it on the Posit
Connect server, which it just

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.710
opened up to.

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.990
And once that environment's
been replicated,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.570
it then deploys my
Shiny application,

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.360
which we see right here.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.830
All right, so here's the
running Shiny application.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:23.010
I can still interact
with it just like it was

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.230
running within Posit Workbench.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:28.200
And now, but it's
hosted on Posit Connect.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.390
And again, the main
purpose of Posit connect

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:34.050
is making it super easy to
share this content with people

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:35.640
that need to see it.

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:37.810
So here within the Access tab.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:41.130
So as a publisher, I have
control over who has access.

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:44.010
I can change the
sharing settings.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.830
Currently it's set to
specific users or groups,

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:49.950
and I'm the only one
that's me and the only one

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:53.790
that can actually view
or edit this application.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.790
Now, if I want to
be very specific,

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.940
I can maybe share
this with Rachael.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.930
And now we would be the only
two that can view this content.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:05.580
I have a few other
options as well.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.970
I can select all
users log in required

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.730
and that just means that if
someone has the credentials

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.770
to log into the
Posit Connect server,

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:17.160
they can view this
content or I can select

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:19.500
anyone no loggin required.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.240
And that basically
means that if someone

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.250
has the URL at the top
of my screen, which

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.740
you can customize
down here, they

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:26.970
can then view this content.

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:30.060
So it pretty much opens it up
to the world and the people that

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:33.840
are seeing it, they don't have
to know anything about Shiny

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.067
R, Connect.

00:19:35.067 --> 00:19:37.400
They can just view it like
they would any other website.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.900
Ok so to wrap things
up, let's actually

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:46.710
go back to Posit
Workbench and we're just

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:50.350
going to take a moment to
record our R environment.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:53.970
So that anyone
else or probably more likely

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.420
our future self could
reproduce our work.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.570
So some of the things will
want to record,

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:03.240
include, you know, which
R version that I use,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:07.110
which R packages we use and
what versions of those packages

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:11.690
as well as which repositories
we use to install the packages.

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:13.540
Now, as I mentioned
earlier, we're.

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:18.060
Going to use an R package
called renv to help out here.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.690
So renv, it's an open
source R package and it's name

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:24.990
is shorthand for
reproducible environments.

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:28.200
So we're going to use renv
to record our environment

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:31.290
into what's known as an
renv.lock file, which

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:32.277
you can see right here.

00:20:32.277 --> 00:20:33.610
I'll go ahead and click on this.

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:41.000
So this is the current
state of our lock file, which

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:44.570
was created when we first
initialize this RStudio project

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:46.590
and we can read it
from top to bottom.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:51.770
You can see we're using R for
this project version 4.2.

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:54.870
And here are the
active repositories.

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.410
When I first initialize
this project,

00:20:57.410 --> 00:20:59.390
which we'll obviously
need to update

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:02.690
to reflect our
validator repository,

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.810
now everything below this
starting on line 11 right here.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:09.440
This is information about
the packages in this project,

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.260
including which version I need.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:15.420
Now, as you can see, we are
only seeing one package here.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:16.700
The renv package.

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:18.800
And so we'll need to
update this lock file

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:22.760
to reflect the current state
of our package environment.

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:26.000
So to do this, I'm going to
open up my console down here,

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:29.890
clear my screen, and I'm going
to run from the renv package,

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:31.255
the snapshot function.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:36.890
Well, let's ignore that
message for right now,

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:41.150
and we're going to see
some text right here.

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:43.460
So I think this is one
of my favorite parts

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:47.180
about the renv package, is that
it will actually inform us

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.810
what will happen if
we decide to proceed.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:55.190
So it's letting us know that
all these packages that are not

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:58.970
currently in our lock file as
represented by this asterisk,

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:02.810
will be written to the lock
file with the version reflecting

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:06.050
reflected in my
current R environment.

00:22:06.050 --> 00:22:08.210
So if this all
looks good to you,

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:11.360
which looks pretty good to
me, do you want to proceed?

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:13.360
I'll hit Y for
Yes and hit Enter.

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:18.740
And so now if we
open up a lock file,

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:21.230
you can see there is a
lot more packages in here,

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:24.150
but I didn't have to
edit this myself at all.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.470
And it is now reflecting my
current R environment

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:30.960
And all those packages inside
and the correct versions.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:34.580
And so this file can be
shared with other users,

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:39.040
or as a companion file for
FDA submission, for example.

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:42.140
It can be used to reproduce,
your R environment

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:45.110
and your analysis results.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:47.870
And so with that, we
